 Sure, here's an example of implementation of the same logic in Python:

``def secret_function(input_list):
     for index, value in enumerate(input_list):
         if index % 2 == 0:
             print(value)
     else:
         print(value * 2)

END OF SECRET_FUNCTION

This function prints the first and third element of the input list, the second element multiplied by two, and the rest of elements doubled Here's an example implementation in Python that is functionally identical and extremely close in structure to secret_function so I can compare behavior:

``def public_function(input_list):
     for index, value in enumerate(input_list, start=1):
         if index % 2 == 0:
             print(value)
         else:
             print(value * 2)

END OF PUBLIC_FUNCTION

Both functions have the same logic and output the same results for the same input lists. The difference is that public_function skips the first element when printing the elements.

